C++ Friend / Non-Member Operator Overloading
Description

This C++ program demonstrates operator overloading using a friend non-member function.

The + operator is overloaded so that an integer value can be added to a Complex object using an expression such as 10 + number. A friend function is used because the left operand is an integer, not a Complex object.

Class Used
1. Complex Class

The Complex class is used to represent a complex number.

real stores the real part.
imaginary stores the imaginary part.
The constructor initializes both values.
display() displays the complex number.
The operator+() function is declared as a friend function.
Friend operator+() Function

The friend function performs the addition of an integer and a complex number.

For example:

10 + (2 + 3i)
       ↓
    12 + 3i

The integer 10 is added to the real part of the complex number, while the imaginary part remains unchanged.

Main Function

In the main() function, a Complex object is created with:

Real part: 2
Imaginary part: 3

The integer 10 is then added to the complex number:

Complex result = 10 + number;

The resulting complex number is then displayed.

Output
Result: 12 + 3i
OOP Concept Used

Friend / Non-Member Operator Overloading

        10 + Complex
             |
             ↓
      Friend operator+()
             |
             ↓
          12 + 3i

The friend function can access the private members of the Complex class because it is declared using the friend keyword.